Unreleased
Changes of results:
* Each Cpos/Cneg solve starts from its weights of the previous iteration, and the least squares steps of L2_SVM_MFN are solved exactly (Cholesky on the normal equations) instead of by CGLS.
* The Cpos/Cneg pair of each fold is fixed after three iterations, and training stops when the estimated positives grow by less than 0.1% over two iterations. --maxiter remains the upper limit.
* The I-spline PEP fit is solved exactly by an active-set method. The trust-region solver stopped at its iteration limit. PEPs change by up to about 0.06; calibration on synthetic data is unchanged.
* Results no longer depend on the number of threads: all sort comparators are total orders, with the input line as the last tie-break. Only ties are affected.
* New option --classic-training restores the previous training: fixed number of iterations, full grid in every iteration, CGLS solves from zero weights.
* New option --num-folds N sets the number of cross-validation folds (default 3). With N = 1 the SVM is trained and evaluated on the same PSMs, for inputs too small to split; its q values and PEPs are optimistic. It is never switched on automatically; when no test fold reaches the test FDR, a hint suggests it or a larger --testFDR.
* New experimental option --merge-normalization test|train|train-decoys|none chooses how the scores of the cross-validation bins are put on a common scale before the merge: by the score at q = --trainFDR and the median decoy of each test bin (test, the previous behaviour and the default with up to three folds), by the same two scores of the fold's training set (train), by the mean and standard deviation of the training-set decoys (train-decoys), or not at all (none). The training-set anchors exist for small test bins, and only test checks each bin's separation, so runs with many folds no longer stop at tiny test bins. With more than three folds (--num-folds) the default is train-decoys, since the anchors of test are noisy in the smaller test bins: on synthetic sets of 10,000 and 20,000 PSMs, test at 10 folds accepted 312 and 186 fewer correct targets at q < 0.01 than 3 folds, with a mean false discovery proportion at q < 0.01 of 1.35% on the 10,000-PSM sets, and train-decoys at 10 folds 33 and 36 more, at 0.95% and 1.03%. With -N only test rescales the scores of all PSMs once more. --static, which rescales nothing, ignores the scheme, also with -N and where the second chance keeps the given weights: the bins are merged, and -N scores all PSMs, as with test. With one fold or RESET the order of the PSMs is the same with every scheme. With one fold the training set holds the test bin's PSMs, so train takes the anchors of test and, except with -N, gives its results; train-decoys and none change the scale of the scores and, for separate searches, can change the pi0 estimate, and with it the q values and PEPs, too, as only test and train estimate the pi0 of the bin's PSMs before the merge, which draws random numbers. RESET estimates no pi0, so there only the scale changes.
* The direction check gets a second chance, and a new experimental option --direction-check bins|second-chance|pooled chooses the check. bins, the previous check, counts the targets with q < --testFDR within each test bin, which needs about 1/testFDR targets above the bin's best decoy, so small inputs (about 1,000 to 5,000 PSMs) and many folds fall back to the default direction even where the trained SVMs separate well. bins stays the default. second-chance, an option, keeps that check and its merge wherever it passes; only where it would fall back, each test bin is scored with its own fold's SVM, the bins are rescaled by their training-set decoys and merged, and the trained SVMs are kept if this out-of-fold list holds targets with q < --testFDR and no fewer than the fallback would report (the folds' default vectors, or the vector of --init-weights, which a fallback restores, merged with the run's scheme, test up to three folds, and counted at --testFDR), and the sign check of --default-direction passes. Such a run is merged with train-decoys, or with the scheme given by --merge-normalization, whose list then has to be usable as well; otherwise the run falls back as before, also where a list cannot be used: a bin that cannot be rescaled, or targets and decoys that separate so well in the merged list, in its best PSM per spectrum (-Y), in its best PSM per peptide (the peptide level) or in a test bin whose pi0 the merge estimates (test, --static) that the run would end with "too good separation" (under --no-terminate, which would not end the run, such a list is treated as unusable as well). One line on stderr gives the two counts and the decision; under --override it changes nothing. pooled decides on the merged list alone, with the pi0 of the final list, falls back where that list cannot be used, and merges with train-decoys unless a scheme is given (test is an error). The merged counts leave the random number generator untouched, so they do not move the pi0 estimates of separate searches. Under --init-weights a reset restores the given weights, so the second chance compares the trained SVMs with them, merged with the run's scheme. On synthetic sets of 1,000 to 10,000 PSMs (10 to 50 features, exact target-decoy calibration, 3 and 10 folds; 400 sets with the -w of an earlier run on the same PSMs, 300 with the -w of an independent 20,000-PSM set of the same kind), bins failed in 97-98% of the runs with weights from the same PSMs and in 84-93% with independent weights. The second chance kept the trained SVMs in 28-39% of the failed runs with weights from the same PSMs (10-12% where the earlier run had kept its trained SVMs, so that its -w held trained weights rather than a default vector) and in 13-29% with independent weights; compared with the default vectors, as before, it kept them in 78-89%. The kept runs had a mean false discovery proportion at q < 0.01 of 0.8-1.6% with weights from the same PSMs and 1.2-1.9% with independent weights (2.2% at 1,000 to 2,000 PSMs and 10 folds), against 0.9-1.3% for runs without --init-weights whose check passes, and they gained 33-47 correct targets per set at q < 0.01 over bins with weights from the same PSMs and 2-8 with independent weights. Weights trained on the same PSMs have seen every test bin, so a fallback to them is optimistic with every check: with the trained -w of an earlier run, the check failed in 94-98% of the runs, and those that fell back had a mean false discovery proportion at q < 0.01 of 4.3% and 2.7% (concatenated and separate searches) at 3 folds, up to 5.4% at 3,000 to 5,000 PSMs, and 2.3% and 2.0% at 10 folds, against 1.0-1.1% for the earlier run on the same sets, whose correct targets at q < 0.01 they exceeded by 9-16% with 2.0 to 3.9 times as many incorrect ones. With independent weights the fallback gave 0.7-0.9%. With one fold, as with RESET, all three checks are the same; with -N the check uses the PSMs trained on, and with --static the merged raw scores.
* qvality estimates PEPs by I-spline regression of the target q values, as Percolator does; --pava-pep, --tdc-pep and --rank-pep select the other monotone regressions, and --irls-pep the previous P-IRLS method.
* New option --tdc-pep (Percolator and qvality): PEPs from a monotone fit of the decoy rate p on the score, PEP = p/(1-p), scaled by pi0 and the target/decoy ratio for separate searches. The default remains the regression of the target q values.
* Nested cross-validation (--nested-xval-bins > 1, and RESET) retrains each fold from its weights of the previous iteration instead of from zero, as the grid solves do. The solves converge to the same solution up to the solver's tolerance: byte-identical outputs on 500k-PSM sets and on 1M PSMs with RESET, PEPs within 1e-8 on 1M_marked with --nested-xval-bins 3.
* -z elastase and -z glu-c check the PSM peptides with the rules that digest the FASTA database, which are also Crux's: elastase cuts after A, L, I or V, Glu-C after D or E, neither before P (before: elastase after L, V, A or G, Glu-C after E only). With -f on 60,000 human PSMs, 3 (elastase) and 11 (Glu-C) protein identifiers change.
* Picked protein: a decoy protein wins an exact score tie with its target, as at PSM level; before, the target always won. Only exact ties change.
Fixes:
* --override: when the direction check failed, all test bins were scored with the first fold's SVM, which was trained on the other bins. Each bin is now scored with its own fold's SVM. Not with -N, which after a failed check scores all PSMs with the averaged weights, also with --override: with --merge-normalization test, the default up to three folds, the bins are still scored with the first fold's SVM there, as after a reset (see the next entry), and these runs are unchanged.
* After a direction reset, all test bins were scored with the default vector chosen on the first fold's training set, which holds the other bins, and the default vectors kept their folds' trained bias. Each bin is now scored with the default vector chosen on its own fold's training set, with bias 0, and the weights written with -w describe the scores of every fold. With --init-weights a reset gives every fold the given weights instead, the same vector in all folds. Only runs that reset change, and only where the folds choose different features or signs; otherwise only the bias of the second and later folds in the -w file changes. Not with -N, which after a reset scores all PSMs with the averaged weights of the folds, rescaled by the merge with the anchors of each bin: with --merge-normalization test, the default up to three folds, the bins are still scored with the first fold's vector there. A bin with no target at q < --trainFDR is anchored at its best score, so with its own fold's vector the real score, with its long tail, got a smaller factor than a noise feature that another fold chose, and the noise feature dominated the average (300k-PSM Gumbel-null set, -N 60000 -S 2: 13,243 instead of 26,839 PSMs at q < 0.05, 12,594 instead of 25,475 of them correct). These -N runs change only in the bias in the -w file.
* The cross-validation split filled the folds with whole spectra but counted their capacity in PSMs, so with uneven spectra or many folds (--num-folds) a fold could stay empty and the run stopped, although there were more spectra than folds. Every fold now gets a spectrum; splits that left no fold empty are unchanged. Known limitation: the split counts a new spectrum where the scan number changes between neighbouring PSMs, so neighbouring spectra of different files with the same scan number count as one, and an input with hardly more spectra than folds can still leave a fold empty.
* --train-best-positive corrupted the training folds with nested cross-validation, which RESET also uses.
* RESET: the pseudo-target labels differed between runs, and a single fold led to a division by zero in the stopping rule.
* --quick-validation with --nested-xval-bins > 1 retrained with a Cpos/Cneg pair that had not been evaluated.
* -N read PSMs with labels other than 1 and -1 as decoys. They are ignored, as without -N.
* -Q/--pepxml-output has written nothing since the XML output was removed; it now prints a warning.
* A non-finite experimental mass (nan, inf) is rejected with an error that names the PSM; it broke the ordering of PSMs.
* A failed load of several input files (unreadable input, a header with fewer columns than the PSMs) left the temporary concatenated file behind.
* With a single cross-validation bin (--num-folds 1, RESET), scores that cannot be rescaled are kept as they are instead of ending the run, so inputs of one to three spectra complete.
* Multiple input files: infinite loop when a column was missing from the widest header; the temporary directory is now removed.
* Out-of-bounds read in ProteinProbEstimator::lastProteinInGroup; uninitialised Peptide reference count (leak in protein digestion); leaked PSMs in the second pass of -N; 32-bit overflow of SVM buffer sizes above about 70 million active rows.
* A plain cmake configure built without optimisation. Release is now the default.
* -N scored the PSMs of the training subset with the averaged weights of folds that had trained on them, so at the top of the list the decoys no longer modelled the incorrect targets. These PSMs now keep the score of the fold in which they were test PSMs, mapped to the mean and standard deviation of the other PSMs' scores. Where the direction check fails, after a reset and with --override, all PSMs are scored with the averaged weights, as before. Weak 500k-PSM set (--shift-scale 0.45), pooled observed FDR at q < 0.01: -N 300k and 400k 0.0123 -> 0.0109 (full cross-validation 0.0107), -N 100k 0.0125 -> 0.0111.
* With --init-weights, a failed direction check fell back to the default direction (the best single feature) instead of the given weights; it now restores them in every fold. On a 500k-PSM set with LDA weights, 0 -> 768 PSMs at q < 0.01 (observed FDR 0.0117). Weights trained on the same PSMs make that fallback optimistic: with the -w of an earlier run on synthetic sets of 1,000 to 10,000 PSMs, the runs that fell back had a mean false discovery proportion at q < 0.01 of 2.0-4.3% (see --direction-check above).
* RESET: the --tdc-pep and --irls-pep PEPs ignored that each remaining decoy stands for two incorrect targets and were half as large as they should be; their odds are now scaled by the decoy factor. Mean PEP of the targets at q < 0.01, 500k-PSM set with an observed FDR of 0.0097: --tdc-pep 0.0046 -> 0.0093, --irls-pep 0.0050 -> 0.0099. The default PEPs and the q values are unchanged.
* --version printed the usage and an error; it now prints the version for percolator and qvality. A lone "-" set --tdc-pep (--irls-pep in qvality); it is now an argument.
* A cross-validation split without training PSMs ran the SVM solver on empty arrays (undefined behaviour); the solver returns zero weights, and a warning names the split.
* -v 3 reported the training set sizes of the previous iteration.
* Library use: about 20 process-wide settings (the seed, PEP and protein flags, file and feature names) carried over from one run to the next in the same process, which changed results and could crash; constructing a Caller resets them.
Input and output checks (most of these inputs gave wrong or missing results with exit status 0 before):
* Feature normalisation factors and trained SVM weights that are not finite stop the run with the feature's name (two Xcorr values of 1e308 made every score NaN).
* The header is checked against the first PSM line: the features must end at the Peptide column. Errors in a multi-file input name the file and its line; a file with only a header is skipped with a warning; label warnings stop after 1,000 lines.
* A peptide without the flanks X.PEPTIDE.Y is used as it is, with one warning per run (it lost two characters at each end, which merged distinct peptides; one shorter than 2 characters aborted the run). A number in the peptide column (an extra feature column) is an error, as is an unflanked peptide with protein inference.
* A weights file (-W) must match the features of the input by name and order when it has a line of names, and hold one finite raw weight per feature plus m0, with at least one feature weight that is not 0: weights that are all 0 cannot order the PSMs, and a run that fell back to them ended because the bins could not be rescaled ("Cannot rescale scores", "no spread").
* Output files and standard output are checked after writing, so a full disk is an error. An output file that is an input or another output is refused (-m x.pin x.pin emptied the input).
* qvality: the input files must open and hold finite numbers (a missing null file reported every target as correct).
* Warnings for a concatenated search with several candidates per spectrum, which is detected as separate searches (suggests -Y), and for -Y with -I concatenated, which ignores -Y.
Speed and memory (output unchanged unless listed above):
* Input parsing with std::from_chars and buffered output.
* SVM: solver buffers reused, training rows stored contiguously and shared by the grid, row-grouped kernels, count-only grid evaluation in parallel over all pairs, parallel search for the initial direction.
* PSMs in a block store with a string arena and interned protein names; ScoreHolder 56 to 40 bytes; PSMDescription without vtable; feature rows and cross-validation data freed after training; PSM-level PEPs only computed when written; I-spline normal equations accumulated without an N x 200 design matrix.
* 1M synthetic PSMs, 3 threads: 155 s and 1.75 GB before, about 24 s and 1.0 GB now.
* Stage timing at -v 3; benchmark and calibration scripts in tests/benchmark (Python standard library only).
* The changes of round two, measured on their integration (329d347) against pull request #404 (b986955), 3 threads, two interleaved runs each on a shared 4-core machine; the PSM and peptide files are identical. 1M_marked (1M synthetic PSMs): 26.9 and 25.4 s -> 15.7 and 14.9 s wall, 62 and 60 s -> 36 and 34 s CPU, peak RSS 1.05 -> 0.88 GB. A 4M-PSM synthetic set: 149 and 130 s -> 65 and 67 s wall, 285 and 295 s -> 146 and 152 s CPU, 3.87 -> 3.31 GB. With --reset-algorithm, 1M: 8.6 and 8.4 s -> 4.3 and 4.6 s, 0.59 -> 0.48 GB; 4M: 39.4 and 39.7 s -> 20.4 and 19.9 s, 2.37 -> 1.81 GB.
* SVM training: the Cpos/Cneg pairs train without the ordered clause; the grid evaluation counts nothing for a fold that trains a single pair and otherwise counts the targets by partial selection; the initial direction reads the features in memory order and counts by partial selection; the line search sorts its breakpoints chunk by chunk; a thread reuses its solver buffers; the kernels prefetch the training rows; nested cross-validation retrains from the previous weights.
* Sorting: parallelSort, a portable in-place OpenMP sort, replaces GCC's parallel mode, so builds with libc++ and MSVC sort in parallel too; test sets already in score order are not sorted again; q values and p values are computed on the sorted PSMs without copies.
* Input and output: separate searches are detected by sorting scan ids, and -N samples spectra with a flat hash table; the protein decoy prefix is found without a string per column; result lines are written with std::to_chars, and numbers parsed with std::from_chars or a fast path where it is missing; the fields before the features are read once; PSMDescription takes 56 instead of 88 bytes; qvality no longer flushes after every line.
* Memory: the fold data are released before the test sets are merged (1M peak 1,050,840 -> 1,001,564 KB). New option --in-place-training trains the SVMs on the feature rows instead of copies: peak RSS -27% to -30% at 1M to 10M PSMs for 15-22% more CPU time.
* RESET selects the best PSM per peptide with one lookup per PSM (4M PSMs: peptide selection 6.2 -> 2.5 s), and --composition-match groups the PSMs without nested maps (4M PSMs: whole run 111.6 -> 75.8 s).
* Protein inference with -f: the FASTA database is read and digested once into a flat peptide index (human Swiss-Prot: 8.6-9.1 -> 1.6-1.9 s, peak RSS 329 -> 121 MB); proteins of 39,999 residues or more no longer end the run.
* The -v 3 stage lines also report the resident set size, its peak and the heap in use (Linux).
Dependencies and removed code:
* Boost and Eigen are no longer needed.
* Removed: libsvm, the Mayu protein FDR estimator, ResultHolder, SqtSanityCheck, Logger, Analytics (--no-analytics is still accepted), unused dblas routines, src/converters/CMakeLists.txt.
* The dblas library is replaced by inline level-1 BLAS helpers with the same results; the FASTA classes adapted from Crux (Database, Protein, Peptide, ProteinPeptideIterator and others, about 5,000 lines) by ProteinDigest; the standalone picked-protein program, which no longer compiled, is removed. Stale CMake Find modules and link libraries, unused macros, getters and protein scoring branches are removed.
* Builds no longer define _GLIBCXX_PARALLEL.
Build, packaging and CI:
* The unit tests use an installed GoogleTest before downloading one (USE_SYSTEM_GTEST), and only they link the threads library. The system tests run with ctest on the build tree; Python 3 is optional.
* Packages: the .deb declares its dependencies, package names carry the architecture, gtest_unit is no longer installed. macOS builds target macOS 12 and link libomp statically where possible, with a warning when OpenMP is missing; the release is universal. Windows links the C++ runtime statically and ships vcomp140.dll. The portable Linux binary is built for glibc 2.28 (RHEL 8). The Docker image ships qvality.
* CI: an ASan and UBSan job (unit and system tests, 30 option combinations), a thread-determinism job (1 against 4 threads, 192 files) and a warning budget.
* Builders: unused scripts removed, option strings fixed, packages of removed code no longer installed.
* tests/benchmark: make_synthetic_pin.py follows the target-decoy assumptions (its concatenated mode had 35% incorrect targets against 50% decoys) and writes the true PEPs of PSMs and peptides; true_pep.py, oracle.py, Brier and Hosmer-Lemeshow statistics; compare_results.py joins peptide rows on the peptide. Its Gaussians have equal variances, so it cannot detect a misspecified PEP tail.
Interface changes (Crux and other library users):
* PSMDescription: proteinIds replaced by proteins(); getId, getFullPeptide and getFullPeptideSequence return std::string_view; the strings live in SetHandler's PsmStore; deletePtr and the virtual functions removed; new setLineNr/getLineNr.
* DataSet::readPsm takes a PsmStore, and a DataSet no longer deletes its PSMs. SetHandler owns its DataSets through std::unique_ptr (push_back_dataset).
* SetHandler::readTab, readAndScoreTab, writeTab and normalizeFeatures and CrossValidation::preIterationSetup and postIterationProcessing take references.
* SanityCheck's static setters are member functions; SqtSanityCheck removed.
* Normalizer::getNormalizer, resetNormalizer and setType replaced by Normalizer::create; Enzyme::createEnzyme returns std::unique_ptr.
* CGLS and L2_SVM_MFN take std::vector arguments; vector_double and vector_int removed; AlgIn is a value type.
* Scores::calcPep takes a fourth argument (tdc); InferPEP::tdc_to_pep takes an optional odds factor; PosteriorEstimator has an estimateMonotonePEP member.
* Scores::getInitDirection sets every element of the direction vector to 0 before it sets the chosen feature, the bias included.
* Scores::merge takes a MergeNormalization scheme and per-bin ScoreScale anchors (both defaulted to the previous behaviour); Scores::normalizeScores is built on the new getScoreScale, calcScoreScale and applyScoreScale; postMergeStep takes an optional flag that turns a too good separation into pi0 = 1 and returns whether the separation is too good; new Scores::countMergedTargets (-1 for a merged list that cannot be used, also by the lists of -Y and the peptide level that its ReportedLists argument names, and by a test bin that separates too well where merge checks each bin: with test and without rescaling) and mergeNormalizationName; Scores::getInitDirection is const. CrossValidation has setMergeNormalization, usedMergeNormalization and the static defaultMergeNormalization (test up to three folds, train-decoys with more).
* SanityCheck: setDirectionCheck (DirectionCheck BINS, SECOND_CHANCE, POOLED), setMergeScheme, setReportedLists, setSecondChanceMergeScheme (the scheme of a run whose trained SVMs the second chance keeps, whose list it then also checks) and secondChanceKept. Its default is BINS, the previous check; setDirectionCheck(SECOND_CHANCE) lets validateDirection keep trained weights whose per-bin count fails. PseudoRandom::getSeed returns the generator state that setSeed restores.
* Globals: logger and stream redirection functions removed. Timer::getStartTimeStr returns std::string. ScoreHolder has no virtual destructor and no p field.
* Normalizer::setSet and updateSet throw MyException for factors that are not finite; new FeatureNames::describeFeature. PSMDescription::getPeptideSequence(View) return an unflanked peptide unchanged; new PSMDescription::hasFlanks and appendProteins, DataSet::resetWarnings, readPsmHeader, readPsmBody and PsmHeader.
* PSMDescription stores its id, peptide and protein list as one record written by PsmStore::storeStrings(psm, id, peptide, proteins); the constructor from a peptide, setId, setPeptide and setProteins, and PsmStore::storeString and storeProteins, are removed.
* New closeOutputFile and flushStandardOutput (MyException.h); ProteinProbEstimator::printOut and PosteriorEstimator::finishStandalone throw MyException on a write error. Caller::parseOptions throws for an output that is an input or another output (checkOutputFiles). Constructing a Caller resets the process-wide settings, so a library user sets them after the constructor; new PosteriorEstimator::getReversed and resetToDefaults.
* CommandLineParser::defineOption treats an empty short name as NO_SHORT_OPT and detects duplicate names; parseArgs keeps a lone "-" as an argument; error no longer prepends the usage text. ValidateTabFile::concatenateMultiplePINs takes an optional std::vector<InputSegment>*; new SetHandler::setInputSegments, describeLine, getSampledLines, getSampledSpectra and getSampledSpectraWithRepeatedLabel. SetHandler::readAndScoreTab takes the fold weights and the (line, fold) list of -N (default empty); CrossValidation has setRecordTestFolds, getTestFolds and getRawFoldWeights.
* Scores::FeatureRows is a vector of FeatureRow words; countTargetsBelowFdr(rows, w, ...) takes a CountBuffers; new countTargetsBySelection and isSortedByScore; the protected calcQvals overload with a vector of pairs is replaced by getTargetPValues. Scores::merge empties the bins it merges, and postIterationProcessing releases the training sets and SVM inputs, so train() cannot follow it; preIterationSetup empties the full set at the split.
* L2_SVM_MFN has an overload with a SolverWorkspace (prepare(m, n, cgls)); AlgIn(n, rowsInPlace), AlgIn::reserve and row(i) replace the public rows; FeatureMemoryPool has setSvmRowsInPlace, rowStride, hasInterceptColumn and canTrainInPlace; CrossValidation::setSvmRowsInPlace. New header ParallelSort.h. CompositionSorter::generateCompositionSignature takes a std::string_view. PosteriorEstimator::estimatePEP takes an odds factor (default 1). TabReader has readSimpleDouble, a constructor from a character range, position and lineEnd. SanityCheck::resetDirection restores the --init-weights vector.
* Libraries: libdblas.a no longer exists; libpicked_protein.a no longer contains Option, Globals and MyException, so libperclibrary.a is linked after it; perclibrary no longer links Threads::Threads; Percolator's libraries are not compiled with -D_GLIBCXX_PARALLEL, which Crux need not and should not define. PickedProteinCaller::initConstraints takes Enzyme::EnzymeType and PickedProtein::DigestType, and greeter and parseOptions are removed with the PercolatorCrux namespace; the PickedProteinInterface constructor has no pvalueCutoff; ProteinInferenceMethod, Scores::getQvaluesBelowLevel, ProteinProbEstimator::countTargets, countDecoys and isTarget, several FeatureNames getters and Globals.h macros are removed.
* On macOS perclibrary is compiled for macOS 12.0 unless Crux passes CMAKE_OSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET; on Windows it uses the static runtime (/MT) unless -DUSE_MSVC_STATIC_RUNTIME=OFF or CMAKE_MSVC_RUNTIME_LIBRARY is given.

v3.09
* Replaced I-spline rank-based PEP calibration with score-based I-spline calibration as the new default; use --rank-pep to opt back to the previous rank-based behaviour. (#403)
* Added I-spline PEP calibration using a trust-region reflective constrained ridge regressor (ISplineTRRRegressor), replacing PAVA post-processing as the default smoother; use --pava-pep to revert to PAVA. (#399)
* Removed XML/XSD I/O support, which was incompatible with modern C++ toolchains. (#399)
* Fixed a bug where PEP values could exceed 1.0. (#394)
* Fixed a variable scope error in Set::reindexTo. (#402)
* Raise an error when tab characters are found in a non-tab-separated protein field. (#380)
* Check that input files exist before attempting decoy prefix detection. (#397)
* Replaced CentOS with AlmaLinux 9 in CI via container build; updated GitHub Actions to Node.js 24 compatible versions and Boost 1.87.0. (#399)

